  "verdict": "On skewed 100-byte KV reads the block-indexed codecs pay a whole 4 KiB block per value: raw fetches 100 B/get, lz4 1171 B/get and zstd-dict 578 B/get, against 82 B/get for EKBLOOM machine 9 (7.1x less I/O than zstd-dict) and 312 B/get for EKBLOOM's best plan (hot tiles). zstd-dict is the smallest store (0.173x raw) and the fastest small-record decoder (1403 ns vs 431 ns get p50); EKBLOOM machine 9 is 0.903x raw, costs 82 B/get (about the value) and has the tightest get tail (852 ns p99.9 vs 4849 ns). EKBLOOM-opt's hot tiles serve hot gets at 151 ns p50 / 100 B/get and cold gets at 1553 ns p50 / 1150 B/get. Net: on skewed small-value KV, EKBLOOM machine 9 buys a real I/O-amplification and tail tradeoff against the block codecs, but zstd-dict stays smaller and decodes faster - stated plainly."
